<div class="page">
  <h2 class="section-title">Marketing return on investment: Seeking clarity for concept and measurement</h2>
  <p>Received in (received form): <span data-field="submission_date">{{ submission_date }}</span></p>
  <div class="author-list">
    <div class="author-block">
      <span class="author-name">{{ author1.full_name }}</span>
      <span class="author-affiliation">{{ author1.affiliation }}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="author-block">
      <span class="author-name">{{ author2.full_name }}</span>
      <span class="author-affiliation">{{ author2.affiliation }}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="author-block">
      <span class="author-name">{{ author3.full_name }}</span>
      <span class="author-affiliation">{{ author3.affiliation }}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="abstract-section">
    <p><strong>Abstract</strong> As the need for accountable marketing spending continues to grow, companies must develop sound metrics and measures of marketing’s contribution to firm profitability. The leading metric has been return on marketing investment (MROI), following the widespread adoption of ROI metrics in other areas of the organisation. However, the ROI metric in marketing is typically interpreted and used in a variety of ways, which causes ambiguity and suboptimal marketing decision making. This paper seeks to remove the ambiguity around MROI to guide better measurements and analysis of marketing’s contribution to firm profitability. We provide a formal definition of MROI and review variations in the use of MROI that are the root cause of ambiguity in its application. We also discuss the practical implications of MROI and how it can be more transparently described if those providing the estimates used the following form: MROI = (Incremental Revenue - Incremental Cost) / Incremental Cost. We also discuss the limitations of MROI and the various studies that illustrate the various uses of MROI, covering different marketing initiatives and business functions. We also discuss the limitations of MROI and the various studies that illustrate the various uses of MROI, covering different marketing initiatives and business functions. The final section of the paper focuses on the connection between MROI and business objectives. While MROI is a useful metric, it is not a panacea for all marketing problems, and its application must be carefully considered.</p>
  </div>
  <p><strong>KEYWORDS:</strong> ROI, marketing impact, marketing metrics, marketing resource allocation, marketing budgeting</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column-body">
    <div class="column">
      <p>An important responsibility of the marketing function is to enable economic decisions on budgeting and allocating the corporate resources devoted to marketing efforts. The return on marketing investment (MROI), aka return on marketing investment (ROMI), is the metric that is increasingly used to evaluate marketing spending and to guide strategic and tactical decisions. Practitioners and academics agree that if dollars are spent or values are committed to marketing purposes, then the firm should strive to monitor and improve returns to marketing efforts in financial (dollar) denominated</p>
      <p>metrics. MROI is arguably the most widely employed measure of enterprise marketing productivity (output/input), even if it is not universally embraced and implemented as many would wish. As such, it is important to ensure that definitional ambiguity does not plague the ability-to-discuss of itself among marketing professionals. The goal of this paper is to provide a formal definition of MROI and to review the various uses of MROI that are being used and reported by practitioners and academics.</p>
      <p><strong>THE PURPOSE OF THIS PAPER</strong></p>
      <p>Although the history of MROI goes back at least to 1971, and was used at AT&T in the late 1980s (Lenskold, 1989), the metric has evolved significantly. It has no clear genesis. Its adoption was driven by the need to measure the effectiveness of marketing investment, and to use ROI to measure firm and strategic marketing contributions to the CFO and others in the finance function. Part of this desire to demonstrate marketing productivity is related to the need to justify marketing spending, and hence the ambition of its strategic objectives. So it is natural that marketing would strive to speak the same language. Finance also struggles with the concept of marketing ROI, and the need to measure productivity metrics by used finance.</p>
      <p>More specifically, MROI is the dollar-denominated estimate of the incremental financial value to the entity generated by marketing activities, less the cost of those expenditures as a percentage of the same expenditure.</p>
      <p><strong>MROI = (Incremental Financial Value Generated by Marketing - Cost of Marketing) / Cost of Marketing</strong></p>
      <p>Unlike other types of investments, marketing funds are rarely tied up in fixed assets or inventory, and are often spent on intangible assets, such as brand equity, and are not easily valued. Therefore, great care will need to be taken to validate comparisons between the ROI of marketing with other ROI estimates. Some marketing actions are similar to other investments, however, in that in many cases</p>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
      <p>appropriate applications, design necessary analytic measurements, and speed of implementation of sorely needed metrics to assess and improve marketing productivity.</p>
      <p>The history of 194 senior marketing managers and executives, 77 per cent reported that they believe ROI is a very useful measure and 67 per cent also think that market share is very useful. Less than half (49 per cent) reported that they believe ROI is a useful measure for measuring and monitoring their business’s. A major reason why managers may not find ROI useful is that they may not have a clear understanding of the measure. Another possible reason is that the metric has been confused with MROI or ROI. Furthermore, Rogers and Lenskold (1990) suggest that the use of marketing ROI is often confused with the use of marketing ROI in part because rewards are often not directly attributable to the marketing effort. Other reasons for the lack of clarity in the use of MROI include the fact that the metric is often used to justify marketing spending, and not to measure marketing productivity.</p>
      <p>In this paper the authors will:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Provide a formal definition of MROI;</li>
        <li>Review the various uses of MROI in practice;</li>
        <li>Discuss the limitations of MROI and the various studies that illustrate the various uses of MROI, covering different marketing initiatives and business functions;</li>
        <li>Discuss the connection between MROI and business objectives.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>B. Scope/granularity of marketing spending</strong></p>
      <p>MROI measures can assess the financial impact of a single marketing tactic or an integrated marketing campaign. The scope of the marketing effort can range from a single ad campaign, to a multi-channel integrated marketing campaign. This paper will explore the various levels of granularity of marketing spending, from the single ad campaign, to a multi-channel integrated marketing campaign. This paper will explore the various levels of granularity of marketing spending, from the single ad campaign, to a multi-channel integrated marketing campaign.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column-body">
    <div class="column">
      <p>(6) conclude with some suggestions for future work to help marketing achieve a common understanding of how to assess and use its most important summary productivity metric, MROI.</p>
      <p><strong>MROI DEFINED</strong></p>
      <p>MROI is the financial value attributable to marketing activities, less the cost of those activities. MROI is a relative measure, and is not a like the traditional ROI, which is an absolute measure. MROI is a relative measure, and is not a like the traditional ROI, which is an absolute measure. MROI is a relative measure, and is not a like the traditional ROI, which is an absolute measure. MROI is a relative measure, and is not a like the traditional ROI, which is an absolute measure.</p>
      <p>MROI metrics for firm or SBU performance are often used to measure the effectiveness of marketing investment, often leave unspecified the time required to generate the return. Therefore, the application of MROI is highly dependent on the context of the firm, the marketing activity, and the specific objectives of the firm.</p>
      <p>More specifically, MROI is the dollar-denominated estimate of the incremental financial value to the entity generated by marketing activities, less the cost of those expenditures as a percentage of the same expenditure.</p>
      <p><strong>Incremental financial value generated by marketing</strong></p>
      <p>Unlike other types of investments, marketing funds are rarely tied up in fixed assets or inventory, and are often spent on intangible assets, such as brand equity, and are not easily valued. Therefore, great care will need to be taken to validate comparisons between the ROI of marketing with other ROI estimates. Some marketing actions are similar to other investments, however, in that in many cases</p>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
      <p>they generate revenue and profit returns over multiple years, building cumulative impact and creating assets with future value. More importantly, they help identify the situations where marketing activities are most appropriate. The next section addresses these complexities.</p>
      <p><strong>THREE COMMON SOURCES OF VARIATIONS IN MROI CALCULATIONS</strong></p>
      <p>The calculation of MROI is subject to several sources of variation, which can lead to different results. We will discuss three common sources of variations that we have identified in the literature, and how they can be addressed. Our discussion is intended to provide a framework for practitioners to generate transparent and reliable metrics that can be used to assess and report marketing productivity.</p>
      <p><strong>A. Methods for valuation of marketing returns</strong></p>
      <p>The most straightforward of marketing returns used in calculating MROI is the profit margin generated from incremental sales. This is what we have termed a <strong>baseline-lift</strong> approach. This approach assumes that the incremental sales are a reasonable measure of the lift over a baseline of sales that would have occurred in the absence of the marketing activity. A slight variation of this approach is to use the incremental revenue as the return in place of profit. When profit margins are unknown or undisclosed, this calculation is</p>
      <p><strong>B. Scope/granularity of marketing spending</strong></p>
      <p>MROI measures can assess the financial impact of a single marketing tactic or an integrated marketing campaign. The scope of the marketing effort can range from a single ad campaign, to a multi-channel integrated marketing campaign. This paper will explore the various levels of granularity of marketing spending, from the single ad campaign, to a multi-channel integrated marketing campaign. This paper will explore the various levels of granularity of marketing spending, from the single ad campaign, to a multi-channel integrated marketing campaign.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column-body">
    <div class="column">
      <p>a useful interim step to calculating MROI, although it does not have the precision needed to optimise spend levels. When only incremental revenue is known, we still consider it a baseline-lift valuation but we must acknowledge that this is a less precise way to distinguish it from a net profit impact.</p>
      <p>It may also be useful when comparing the marketing productivity of two alternative marketing initiatives for the same product or service.</p>
      <p>The next two forms of valuation are incremental cost and incremental revenue, which are necessary to account for outcomes when sales lift is unknown. The first is a funnel conversion outcome, where the valuation of marketing returns involves projecting incremental sales by applying historical conversion rates. The second is referred to as a comparable cost approach, where the valuation of marketing returns is based on the cost of the marketing activities, rather than the return from the marketing activities.</p>
      <p>We also need two forms of valuation that capture the contribution beyond the immediate sales lift, such as customer equity (CE) and marketing assets. These capture less transparent, but potentially more valuable, outcomes of marketing.</p>
      <p>The same marketing efforts might be valued in a number of different ways.</p>
